{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1790266242,"nonce":"WFTe7lOZZI6I0LdzIWEKJSD2z5c=","target":"5673895c53b7214ed7c811c18b1cb7064788b6641a4d06df5bae0197eea9fd7c","message":"HANDOFF, paused by Dorian mid-investigation (2026-09-24). NO CODE WAS WRITTEN. The work so far is research and design, and all of it is recorded here.\n(This comment first posted by mistake with the room-library handoff text: the two workers shared a scratch file path. That text's own copy is bac4eba's comment b9a8c84; this edit replaces the duplicate.)\n\nBRANCH / WORKTREE / COMMIT\n- Branch spec/b004581-runner, created at 769fdd5 (main's HEAD). It has no commits of its own.\n- Worktree: /home/dorian/projects/rimworld/autorimmer/.claude/worktrees/agent-aeb4656e2794256f9. It is clean.\n- Smoke calls: NONE were made, so usage is 0. The budget of three is untouched.\n\nWHAT IS DONE (read and verified, nothing built)\nI read the spec and its comment, DESIGN (Protocol, Time, Observation, Action, Conventions, and the 722c951, 65e7cf9 and 975973e entries), COCKPIT.md (audit branch) and 975973e with its comments. In the source I read Screen.cs, ScreenSince.cs, ScreenDecisions.cs, the ScreenGauges keys, TimeDriver Start/Teardown/BuildData, StateWatch.cs, JournalVerbs.Read and SaveVerbs. I also read the rwa README, rwa/rwa send/emit/journal, fakebench.py, cockpit/*, PLAY-LOOP, SESSION-START, all of checklists/ and the run contract.\nFor the CLI, I checked the installed `claude --help` (2.1.281). I read the docs as raw markdown: curl https://code.claude.com/docs/en/\u003cpage\u003e.md. The WebFetch summariser contradicted `--help` on --safe-mode, --restricted and --strict-mcp-config, so do not trust it.\n\nRESOLUTION 1, TOOL SURFACE. Decided on the docs; not yet proven by a smoke call.\nThe player gets one generic tool, `verb {op, args}`, from a stdlib MCP stdio server (server name `bench`). The `op` field is an enum built from the live `status` verb's data.verbs, minus a forbidden set:\n- advance, pause and unpause, because the runner is the only driver;\n- journal, because it moves the watermark;\n- look, because it delivers a screen and moves the screen mark;\n- journal-selftest, pawn-fixture and world-fixture, because they are dev-gated fixtures;\n- catalog-dump;\n- anything starting with dev:.\nTwo runner tools sit beside it: `end_stop {notebook, wake, through?, note?}` and a read-only `journal_rows` (below).\nWhy not per-verb tools:\n- The mod publishes no per-verb argument schema, so per-verb tools would need a hand-kept client-side verb table. rwa refuses one on principle, because it rots.\n- Claude Code truncates every tool description at 2,048 chars.\n- 117 schemas add about 7K tokens to the cached prefix for no gain in information over the enum.\nRequired plumbing:\n- Unless ENABLE_TOOL_SEARCH=false is set, Claude Code defers ALL MCP tools. The player would then have to ToolSearch for them, and it has no built-ins. So set it to false.\n- A tool result over the MCP limit is diverted to a file, and the player cannot read files. So every tool declares _meta {\"anthropic/maxResultSizeChars\": 500000}, which is the hard ceiling. Also set MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_TOKENS high. The server refuses, visibly, any reply above 500,000 chars; it never truncates.\n- rwa is called as a subprocess: `rwa send \u003cop\u003e --args-json - --json`. That keeps the envelope byte-exact and every call lands in transcripts/\u003cRWA_RUN\u003e/.\nPlayer command line (all flags verified in --help or the raw docs):\n- `claude -p --model M --system-prompt-file F --tools \"\" --strict-mcp-config --mcp-config F --allowedTools mcp__bench --permission-mode dontAsk --permission-prompts none --setting-sources project --disable-slash-commands --no-session-persistence --max-turns N --output-format stream-json --verbose`, with the prompt on stdin.\n- The last stream line is the same result object that `--output-format json` prints (usage, total_cost_usd, num_turns, permission_denials).\n- system/init lists tools, mcp_servers, plugins, skills, agents and slash_commands, which proves \"no shell\" on every stop.\n- `--tools \"\"` still leaves EndConversation while MCP tools exist. That is expected and harmless.\n\nRESOLUTION 2, CONTEXT ISOLATION. Designed; NOT proven, because no smoke call was made.\nFindings on this box:\n- /home/dorian/CLAUDE.md exists (empty). Any cwd under /home/dorian would load it through parent traversal.\n- ~/.claude/settings.json has a UserPromptSubmit hook, `python3 /home/dorian/apps/claude-usage-guard/usage_guard.py check`. When its alert is on, the hook injects additionalContext (\"Shared Claude usage guard: ... leave room for your friend\"). It never blocks.\n- The same file enables six plugins (context7, playwright, typescript-lsp and others), sets model opus[1m] and sets effort high/xhigh.\n- The user also has claude.ai connector MCP servers.\nSuppression:\n- The player's cwd is outside /home entirely: $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/autorimmer-player/\u003crun\u003e/stop-N/cwd.\n- Environment: CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_CLAUDE_MDS=1 (the docs say this covers all CLAUDE.md, including user, project and auto memory) and CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1.\n- --setting-sources project drops user settings: the hook, the plugins and the model. The empty cwd has no project settings.\n- --strict-mcp-config drops every other MCP server, --disable-slash-commands drops skills, and --system-prompt-file replaces the default prompt with its cwd/env/git sections.\n- Pass --model explicitly, because the user default no longer applies.\nPlanned proof:\n- The system/init event on every stop.\n- On the smoke calls, a local recording proxy set via ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL. The docs say a subscription login stays active behind a base URL if headers pass verbatim, including anthropic-beta. The proxy streams SSE unbuffered and redacts auth headers, and it captures the exact system prompt, tools and first message.\n- Setting a base URL changes the tool-search default, so ENABLE_TOOL_SEARCH=false must be set explicitly either way.\nFLAG FOR DORIAN: isolating user settings means his usage-guard warning no longer reaches the player. The runner should surface it itself, or Dorian decides.\n\nRESOLUTION 3, THE READ GATE. Settled in design.\n- After every advance, the runner reads through the VERB: `rwa send journal`. `rwa journal` reads the FILE and moves nothing. The read starts from the advance's echoed journal_read_watermark with limit 2000, paging until unread_after == 0. This honestly discharges 722c951; the runner never passes unread_ok.\n- The rows go whole into the stop record, since that journal call and its reply are recorded, and into RUNS/\u003crun\u003e/journal-read.ndjson.\n- The player reaches the same rows two ways: the mod's own reading of them in screen.since (which always keeps death, downed, destroyed, letter, red_error and mental_break), and the read-only `journal_rows` tool, served from that cache. Reading through the tool moves no watermark.\n- On an unread-journal refusal, parse read_watermark= from error.detail and page from there.\n- Subtlety: runner-only advances (slices where no player is called) each consume a screen's since window. Their since panels must be merged into the next player stop, or rows vanish from the player's view.\n\nOTHER DESIGN POINTS SETTLED\n- ONE UNTIL MATCHER. TimeDriver.CheckUntilKeys allows one matcher per advance. The runner arms one condition: the player's wake, else the first stop-clock rule in file order. It polls the rest at slice boundaries (timeout_ticks = check_every_ticks, default 2500) against a fresh digest, with runner-side edge tracking.\n- MOD-SIDE NEED: `until:{any:[...]}`, a composite StateWatch. With it the runner needs no slicing.\n- A player stop happens on any mod halt (letter, alert, casualty, destroyed, dialog, error, stalled, condition), on a refusal (bleedout-deadline), on a polled crossing, on the day bound (60000 ticks since the last stop), or when the wake deadline arrives. The only return that calls no player is a runner slice timeout with no crossing.\n- RULES FILE: runner/rules.toml, parsed with tomllib (Python 3.14 here) and re-read at every advance return. On a parse error, keep the last good copy and record the error loudly. Each [[rule]] has:\n  - id;\n  - when, the mod's {path, op, value, edge?, quantify?}. The path is a digest path, as in the mod, or a `screen.` path. An absent screen path counts as quiet, and that is recorded;\n  - remind;\n  - stop_clock, which takes digest paths only;\n  - every = stop | crossing | day | \"N days\";\n  - source = \"file:line\".\n  The rules render into the system prompt, which is cached; fired reminders go in the stop message.\n- Gotchas:\n  - An ordering comparison against null is a mod refusal, and power.battery_days is null whenever there is no deficit, so use `!= null` there.\n  - resources.food_days is -1 when nobody needs food.\n  - The grammar cannot compare two paths, so turn.md's \"meds \u003c colonists\" becomes a constant floor.\n- Paths verified in the source:\n  - resources: food_days, meds, wood, steel;\n  - power: battery_days, nets_with_generator;\n  - construction.stalled_count;\n  - threats: hostiles_active, hostiles_unpardoned;\n  - work_coverage.ok and posture.ok;\n  - screen: stop.kind, colonists.unarmed, gauges.stock.uninstalled, gauges.goals.G1..G6.ok, decisions.total_owed, since.counts.\u003ctype\u003e, since.clock.outside_ticks.\n  `screen.stop.kind == \"look\"` marks a session start.\n- Seed map:\n  - turn.md trip-wires become level rules; food-days-floor also gets stop_clock;\n  - daily.md items fire at dawn (time.hour \u003e= 6, every=day) under one stop-clock \"dawn\" rule;\n  - triggered.md maps onto screen.stop.kind (casualty, dialog), since.counts.death (roster change), hostiles_active == 0 (raid end, every=crossing) and since.clock (time drift);\n  - the contract's standing rules become daily reminders;\n  - rule 5 of the contract is retired by the runner.\n  Trade, Zzzt, toxic-fallout, bill-health and room-enclosure triggers are not expressible; list them in rules.toml as not seeded, and why.\n- Saves: auto-save at the day boundary, threat and casualty stops. Names must be at most 40 chars with no periods (SaveVerbs rejects /\\{}\u003c\u003e:*|!@#$%^\u0026*?.).\n- Player failures: Claude retries internally first (CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_RETRIES, default 10). The runner then backs off at 30s, 60s, 120s and so on, up to a budget. After that it makes a loud halt: save, banner, RUNS/\u003crun\u003e/HALTED, non-zero exit. max-turns or no end_stop falls back to the default wake and records it.\n- The player may pass `through {casualties|news|losses}` reasons in end_stop, for the next advance only.\n- fakebench has no screen, no gate, no watermark and no condition evaluation. Subclass it in runner/test. Do not edit rwa/, which the other worker owns.\n- Scratch files: use a per-worker subdirectory. The scratchpad directory is shared between workers.\n\nNOT DONE: everything built. That covers:\n- the runner, the MCP server and the rules;\n- the seeded rules.toml;\n- the player system prompt;\n- the sim bench and the stub player;\n- the whole-loop test;\n- the recording proxy;\n- `cockpit --stops`, which needs a StopRun giving transcript.Run's step interface over stop records;\n- `runner report`, tokens per stop and per in-game day;\n- runner/README.md and accept/b004581-runner.md;\n- the PLAY-LOOP.md superseded banner and the d2e1229 comment;\n- the three smoke calls.\n\nEXACT NEXT STEPS\n1. Check out spec/b004581-runner in a fresh worktree.\n2. Build these, in order:\n   - runner/bench.py, the rwa subprocess wrapper that records every call;\n   - runner/rules.py, the parser plus an evaluator that mirrors PathWatch semantics;\n   - runner/mcp_server.py;\n   - runner/runner, the play/report/check-rules loop;\n   - runner/player-system.md;\n   - runner/rules.toml.\n3. Build runner/test/: sim.py (a fakebench.Bench subclass with the gate, the watermark, a screen with since, conditions and scheduled events), stubplayer.py (speaks MCP to the real server and emits stream-json with fake usage) and a loop test. The loop test should include a rules edit between two stops, done through a sentinel handshake with no restart.\n4. Build cockpit/stops.py and `cockpit --stops ... --probe`, read-only.\n5. Write runner/test/capture-proxy.py, then spend at most three tiny smoke calls on the fakebench sim with --model sonnet: tools reachable, whole replies, init with no shell, and the captured request proving isolation. Report the usage of each.\n6. Write the README, the accept doc (under 200 lines, commands only), the resolution comments, and the d2e1229 supersession.","files":null}]}